WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.034
Every command you type with the AWS CLI becomes an API call to AWS.

2
00:00:08.034 --> 00:00:12.015
Like this one, which reads a report from a bucket.

3
00:00:12.265 --> 00:00:15.798
Any call can create, change or delete something.

4
00:00:15.798 --> 00:00:19.553
So before S3 does anything, AWS asks two questions.

5
00:00:19.803 --> 00:00:20.784
Who is calling?

6
00:00:20.784 --> 00:00:23.336
And may they do this, on this resource?

7
00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:26.320
The first answer comes from credentials.

8
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.514
An access key ID, a secret, and, for temporary credentials, a session token.

9
00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:34.545
The CLI never sends the secret itself.

10
00:00:34.545 --> 00:00:37.473
It uses the secret to sign each request.

11
00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:42.019
AWS checks the signature with its own copy of the secret.

12
00:00:42.019 --> 00:00:45.033
If they match, AWS knows who is calling.

13
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:47.922
So where should those credentials come from?

14
00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:50.501
The worst place is a long-lived access key.

15
00:00:50.751 --> 00:00:57.350
It works for whoever holds it, copied from a laptop, a log or a git repo, until someone deletes it.

16
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.828
People should sign in through a browser instead.

17
00:01:00.828 --> 00:01:02.173
aws login does that.

18
00:01:02.173 --> 00:01:05.535
It turns your browser sign-in into temporary keys.

19
00:01:05.785 --> 00:01:10.395
It stores temporary credentials and refreshes them every 15 minutes.

20
00:01:10.395 --> 00:01:12.497
No key ever lands on your disk.

21
00:01:13.247 --> 00:01:14.761
Now the second question.

22
00:01:14.761 --> 00:01:17.979
Say the caller has the lab's reports reader policy.

23
00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:22.333
It allows listing the reports, and reading the objects under reports.

24
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:25.623
AWS decides every call the same way.

25
00:01:25.623 --> 00:01:27.311
It starts from deny.

26
00:01:27.561 --> 00:01:29.891
A matching Allow opens the call.

27
00:01:29.891 --> 00:01:34.551
s3:GetObject on reports matches, so the call goes through to S3.

28
00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:36.153
Now try a delete.

29
00:01:36.153 --> 00:01:39.731
Nothing in the policy allows s3:DeleteObject.

30
00:01:39.731 --> 00:01:42.991
So it stays denied, and S3 never sees it.

31
00:01:43.241 --> 00:01:44.509
And one more rule.

32
00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:47.607
An explicit Deny anywhere beats every Allow.

33
00:01:49.057 --> 00:01:51.496
Programs should not hold keys at all.

34
00:01:51.496 --> 00:01:52.748
They assume a role.

35
00:01:52.748 --> 00:01:54.858
And a role carries two policies.

36
00:01:55.108 --> 00:01:59.723
The permissions policy, like the one we just read, says what the role may do.

37
00:01:59.973 --> 00:02:02.390
The trust policy says who may assume it.

38
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.723
Here the Principal ends in root.

39
00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:10.647
That means the whole account: any identity in it whose own policy allows assuming the role.

40
00:02:10.647 --> 00:02:12.665
It does not mean the root user.

41
00:02:12.915 --> 00:02:15.783
aws sts assume-role checks both sides.

42
00:02:15.783 --> 00:02:21.291
Your own policy must allow it, and the role's trust policy must list you.

43
00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:24.211
Then it hands back temporary credentials.

44
00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:26.099
They last an hour by default.

45
00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:28.545
Their key ID starts with ASIA.

46
00:02:28.545 --> 00:02:30.941
Long-lived keys start with AKIA.

47
00:02:30.941 --> 00:02:33.636
So you can tell them apart in a log.
